"""New explanations and examples following the mathematical order of book 5.1–5.2."""
import math
def example(code,title,question,solution):return dict(title=title,question=question,solution=solution,verification=code)
def lesson(key,title,summary,objective,before,ideas,method,mistakes,examples):
    return dict(key=key,id='fp1_polar_'+key,title=title,summary=summary,objective=objective,before=before,ideas=ideas,method=method,mistakes=mistakes,examples=examples)
def bisect_height(c):
    lo,hi=0,math.pi/2
    for _ in range(90):
        mid=(lo+hi)/2
        if (mid+c)*math.cos(mid)-math.sin(mid)>0:lo=mid
        else:hi=mid
    return (lo+hi)/2
phi=bisect_height(3);height=5*math.sin(phi)/(phi+3)
lessons=[
lesson('coordinates','Connecting polar and Cartesian forms',
 'Describe a point by distance and direction, retaining the specified branch.',
 'Convert points and curve equations without losing the pole or introducing an unwanted branch.',
 r'<p>You need radians, exact trigonometric values, quadrants and Cartesian equations. Review <a href="/courses/maths4u-9709-p1/topics/orig_quadratics_completing_square">completing the square</a> for circle equations.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Read the convention before calculating</h3><p>The pole is the origin; the initial line is the positive x-axis. The angle is measured anticlockwise in radians. In this chapter we use \(r\ge0\): an angle producing a negative radius is outside the stated curve, rather than an instruction to plot a different point.</p>
 \[x=r\cos\theta,\qquad y=r\sin\theta,\qquad r^2=x^2+y^2.\]
 <p>For a non-pole point, \(r=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\). Use the signs of x and y to choose the correct quadrant for \(\theta\), then place it in the required angular interval. The equation \(\tan\theta=y/x\) alone does not determine the quadrant and is unavailable when \(x=0\).</p>
 <h3>The pole needs its own check</h3><p>Every allowed angle with \(r=0\) represents the same point. Dividing by r can remove that point; multiplying by r can introduce it into an equation that originally excluded it. Test it in the original description.</p>
 <h3>Change the equation and preserve its domain</h3><p>Multiply by a suitable power of r, use the coordinate identities, and retain sign or sector restrictions. A Cartesian equation obtained by squaring may describe extra branches. Conversely, recovering a non-negative radius from a Cartesian curve can require several disjoint angular intervals.</p>
 <p>For example, \(r^2\cos2\theta=x^2-y^2\) and \(r^2\sin2\theta=2xy\). These identities are algebraic tools, not substitutes for the domain. A line \(\theta=\alpha\) with \(r\ge0\) is a ray; distance from the full line in that direction is a separate concept used later.</p>''',
 r'<ol><li>State the radius convention and angular interval.</li><li>Use the coordinate identities and a quadrant check.</li><li>Convert the full equation, recording any division or squaring.</li><li>Check the pole and write the branch restrictions with the answer.</li></ol>',
 ['An inverse tangent alone can select the wrong quadrant.','Different polar pairs may represent the same point.','Squaring an equation may add a branch.','Check r=0 before cancelling a radial factor.'],
 [
 example('E-C1','1 · Convert a point exactly',r'<p>Convert \((r,\theta)=(4,5\pi/6)\) into Cartesian coordinates.</p>',r'\[x=4\cos\frac{5\pi}{6}=-2\sqrt3,\qquad y=4\sin\frac{5\pi}{6}=2.\]<p>Thus the point is \((-2\sqrt3,2)\). Its signs place it in quadrant II, consistent with the given angle.</p>'),
 example('E-C2','2 · Recover the correct quadrant',r'<p>Find the polar coordinates of \((-5,5\sqrt3)\), using \(r\ge0\) and \(-\pi&lt;\theta\le\pi\).</p>',r'\[r=\sqrt{25+75}=10.\]<p>The reference angle is \(\pi/3\), but the point is in quadrant II. Hence</p>\[(r,\theta)=\left(10,\frac{2\pi}{3}\right).\]<p>The angle \(-\pi/3\) has the same tangent but gives the wrong signs.</p>'),
 example('E-C3','3 · Recognise the whole circle',r'<p>Convert \(r=8\sin\theta\), \(0\le\theta\le\pi\), into Cartesian form and identify the locus.</p>',r'<p>Multiplying by r gives \(x^2+y^2=8y\), so</p>\[x^2+(y-4)^2=16.\]<p>This is the whole circle with centre \((0,4)\) and radius 4. The endpoint angles both give the pole, which lies on the circle. The supplied interval traverses the circle once.</p>'),
 example('E-C4','4 · Select one loop explicitly',r'<p>Convert \(r^2=18\cos2\theta\), \(-\pi/4\le\theta\le\pi/4\), into Cartesian form, including its branch restriction.</p>',r'<p>Multiply by \(r^2\) and use the double-angle identity:</p>\[(x^2+y^2)^2=18(x^2-y^2),\qquad x\ge0.\]<p>The right-hand loop is selected. Both angular endpoints are the pole; it must be retained even though a derivation for \(r&gt;0\) divides by a radial factor.</p>')
 ]),
lesson('sketches','Sketching admissible polar curves',
 'Use a domain, a short table and symmetry to trace the actual curve.',
 'Sketch loops and spirals with correct endpoints, symmetry, radius and non-negative-radius restrictions.',
 r'<p>Review <a href="/courses/maths4u-9231-fp1/topics/fp1_polar_coordinates">coordinate conversion</a> and the signs of sine and cosine.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Build a small set of reliable landmarks</h3><p>Find the allowed angular intervals first. Calculate starting and final radii, zeros, simple intermediate values and the largest radius. Plot coordinate points from \(r\cos\theta,r\sin\theta\), then trace them in increasing angle.</p>
 <h3>Test symmetry algebraically</h3><p>If both angles are in the domain, \(r(-\theta)=r(\theta)\) gives reflection in the x-axis. More generally, \(r(2\alpha-\theta)=r(\theta)\) gives symmetry in the line through the pole at angle \(\alpha\). An algebraic identity is not sufficient if it maps outside the permitted interval.</p>
 <h3>Loops, offsets and multiple traces</h3><p>Zeros may mark successive returns to the pole. For \(r^2=g(\theta)\), real radii require \(g(\theta)\ge0\), and the positive square root is used. For an offset trigonometric radius, check the actual sign before drawing an inner loop. Do not import a signed-radius sketch into this non-negative-radius convention.</p>
 <h3>A spiral need not start at the pole</h3><p>An increasing radius does not make both Cartesian coordinates increase. Label the true starting radius and terminal ray. A sector bounded by an open arc also needs the radial segments joining its endpoints to the pole. An infinite or open angular endpoint may require a limit instead of an endpoint value.</p>
 <p>When a smooth branch reaches the pole, the limiting direction of its position vector gives the tangent line through the pole. Multiple approaches can give different tangent lines. Use the parametrisation to check exceptional cusps or vanishing derivatives.</p>''',
 r'<ol><li>Find the non-negative-radius domain and all pole angles.</li><li>Test symmetry within that domain.</li><li>Calculate endpoints, easy radii and radial extrema.</li><li>Trace the branches once, with equal Cartesian axis scales.</li></ol>',
 ['A non-negative-radius convention can remove some petals.','A maximum radius is not necessarily a maximum height.','A spiral with positive initial radius does not begin at the pole.','An angle endpoint and a distinct geometric point are different counts.'],
 [
 example('E-S1','1 · Keep every admissible interval',r'<p>Describe \(r=6\cos2\theta\), \(-\pi&lt;\theta\le\pi\), using \(r\ge0\).</p>',r'<p>We need \(\cos2\theta\ge0\), giving</p>\[(-\pi,-3\pi/4]\ \cup\ [-\pi/4,\pi/4]\ \cup\ [3\pi/4,\pi].\]<p>These intervals trace two geometric loops along the x-axis, not four signed-radius petals. The greatest radius is 6. The first and last intervals belong to the same left loop across the angular cut.</p>'),
 example('E-S2','2 · Locate the cusp and intercepts',r'<p>Describe a sketch of \(r=3(1-\cos\theta)\), \(0\le\theta\le2\pi\).</p>',r'<p>The radius is non-negative. It vanishes at 0 and \(2\pi\), so those representations give one cusp at the pole. The curve is symmetric in the x-axis. At \(\pi/2,\pi,3\pi/2\), its Cartesian points are \((0,3),(-6,0),(0,-3)\). The maximum radius is 6 at \(\theta=\pi\). Trace the upper half first, then the lower half.</p>'),
 example('E-S3','3 · Exclude negative radii before drawing',r'<p>For \(r=1+4\cos\theta\), choose the maximal interval \([-\beta,\beta]\subset(-\pi,\pi)\) on which \(r\ge0\), and describe its landmarks.</p>',r'\[\beta=\arccos(-1/4).\]<p>The endpoints are the pole, the curve is symmetric in the x-axis, and its largest radius is 5 at angle 0. It crosses the y-axis at \((0,\pm1)\). Outside this interval the formula has negative radius; those points are excluded in this question.</p>'),
 example('E-S4','4 · An arc with a non-zero start',r'<p>Describe \(r=1+2\theta\), \(0\le\theta\le\pi\), labelling both endpoints and the point on the positive y-axis.</p>',r'<p>The radius increases strictly from 1. The endpoints in polar form are \((1,0)\) and \((1+2\pi,\pi)\); at angle \(\pi/2\), the radius is \(1+\pi\). The arc remains in the upper half-plane and never reaches the pole. A bounded sector also includes the radial segment on each endpoint ray.</p>')
 ]),
lesson('intersections','Intersections and changing boundaries',
 'Find common non-pole points, check the pole separately and identify which curve bounds a region.',
 'Determine all admissible intersections and split a composite region at the correct angles.',
 r'<p>Review <a href="/courses/maths4u-9231-fp1/topics/fp1_polar_sketches">sketching</a> and trigonometric equations. Work on intersection and boundary parts now; the following area lesson supplies the integral method for the area parts of these multi-skill questions.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Equal radii describe a common non-pole ray</h3><p>For a non-pole point and a common angular interval, solve \(r_1(\theta)=r_2(\theta)\). If both radii are non-negative, equality of their squares is also valid. Reject angles outside either domain and recover the radius for every retained angle.</p>
 <h3>The pole is a geometric point, not one privileged angle</h3><p>A curve passes through the pole if its radius is zero at any permitted angle. Two curves can share that point at different parameter angles. Thus equal-radius equations alone can miss a common pole. Check it separately, and do not count repeated polar descriptions as separate geometric intersections.</p>
 <h3>Which boundary is needed?</h3><p>On a fixed ray, the common interior extends to \(\min(r_1,r_2)\). A region inside one curve and outside another extends from the inner to the outer radius only where that ordering holds. List all boundary-change angles before constructing an area integral.</p>
 <p>Test a radius in each intervening interval. Intersections at the pole do not always change which non-zero boundary is active. A Cartesian chord such as \(y=h\) has radial equation \(r=h\csc\theta\) where that ray meets the chord; include the relevant angular restriction.</p>
 <h3>Numerical intersections still need a domain argument</h3><p>For an equation with no useful exact root, establish a sign change and, where possible, monotonicity. A decimal approximation without a uniqueness or completeness check does not identify all boundary changes.</p>''',
 r'<ol><li>Use the common angular domain and solve the non-pole equation.</li><li>Check the pole independently and remove duplicate geometric points.</li><li>Recover every intersection radius.</li><li>Test the radius ordering in each interval and identify the required region.</li></ol>',
 ['Never cancel a factor before considering its zeros.','The common pole can occur at different parameter angles.','Inside both curves means the smaller radius on each ray.','A chord boundary is not generally a constant-radius arc.'],
 [
 example('E-I1','1 · A circle meeting a petal',r'<p>Find the non-pole intersection of \(r=3\cos\theta\) and \(r=3\sin2\theta\) in \(0\le\theta\le\pi/2\).</p>',r'<p>For a non-pole point, \(\cos\theta&gt;0\). Dividing \(3\cos\theta=6\sin\theta\cos\theta\) gives \(\sin\theta=1/2\). Therefore</p>\[(r,\theta)=\left(\frac{3\sqrt3}{2},\frac{\pi}{6}\right).\]<p>Both curves also reach the pole. Before the non-pole intersection the petal has smaller radius; afterwards the circle does.</p>'),
 example('E-I2','2 · A common pole at different angles',r'<p>Find all geometric intersections of \(r=4\cos\theta\) and \(r=6\sin\theta\), taking each curve over its full non-negative-radius circle.</p>',r'<p>The pole occurs on the first circle at \(\theta=\pm\pi/2\), and on the second at \(\theta=0,\pi\). It is one common geometric point. At the other intersection, \(4\cos\theta=6\sin\theta\), so \(\theta=\arctan(2/3)\) and</p>\[r=\frac{12}{\sqrt{13}}.\]<p>Its Cartesian coordinates are \((36/13,24/13)\). The two circles intersect in these two points; equating radii at one common angle alone would miss the pole.</p>'),
 example('E-I3','3 · Identify a switched common boundary',r'<p>Compare \(r=2\) and \(r=2(1+\cos\theta)\), \(-\pi\le\theta\le\pi\). Identify the boundary of their common interior.</p>',r'<p>They meet when \(\cos\theta=0\), at \((r,\theta)=(2,\pm\pi/2)\). The circle has no pole, so there is no extra common pole. For \(|\theta|&lt;\pi/2\), the circle has smaller radius; for \(\pi/2&lt;|\theta|\le\pi\), the cardioid has smaller radius. These are the intervals for a common-area calculation.</p>'),
 example('E-I4','4 · Select the admissible algebraic root',r'<p>Find the intersection of \(r=2+\theta\) and \(r=\theta^2\) on \(0\le\theta\le\pi\).</p>',r'\[\theta^2-\theta-2=(\theta-2)(\theta+1)=0.\]<p>Only \(\theta=2\) belongs to the domain, and its radius is 4. Both radii are non-negative, so this is the unique non-pole intersection. The second curve reaches the pole at 0, but the first does not; there is no common pole.</p>')
 ]),
lesson('areas','Polar areas and composite regions',
 'Derive sector areas, split changing boundaries and retain straight-edge corrections.',
 'Set up and evaluate complete area calculations with the correct squared radii, limits and boundary segments.',
 r'<p>Review <a href="/courses/maths4u-9231-fp1/topics/fp1_polar_intersections">intersections</a>, integration and double-angle identities. Use radians in every area integral.</p>',
 r'''<h3>From thin sectors to an integral</h3><p>A narrow sector of angle \(\Delta\theta\) and radius r has approximate area \(\frac12r^2\Delta\theta\). Summing and taking the limit gives</p>
 \[A=\frac12\int_\alpha^\beta r(\theta)^2\,d\theta.\]
 <p>The region is bounded by the arc and its endpoint rays. Check that the parametrisation traces the required region once. If the question supplies \(r^2\), use that expression directly; do not square it again.</p>
 <h3>Between two boundaries</h3>
 \[A=\frac12\int_\alpha^\beta\left(r_{\rm outer}^2-r_{\rm inner}^2\right)\,d\theta.\]
 <p>This is a difference of squared radii, not the square of their difference. Split the calculation whenever the active boundary or angular domain changes. For a common interior use the smaller radius on each ray, with inner radius zero.</p>
 <h3>Integrate efficiently without changing the region</h3><p>Use \(\sin^2u=(1-\cos2u)/2\) and its cosine counterpart. Products with exponentials or logarithms may require integration by parts; a substitution can simplify a squared-radius expression. Verify an antiderivative by differentiation before substituting its limits.</p>
 <h3>Account for a straight boundary</h3><p>A chord need not follow a ray. You can integrate its radial equation, or subtract a triangle from a central sector. Draw the actual boundary and check the sign of the correction. A polar sector is not automatically the Cartesian area under the arc.</p>
 <h3>Recovering a parameter or a dividing ray</h3><p>Write the full area as a function of the unknown parameter or upper angle, then solve the required area equation. For equal-area or prescribed-ratio divisions, compare the correct cumulative area with the total, retaining the allowed interval.</p>''',
 r'<ol><li>Describe the region and all boundary-change angles.</li><li>Write one half times the integral of the appropriate squared radius difference.</li><li>Integrate each piece and keep the same limits.</li><li>Check positivity, geometry, scale and any recovered parameter restrictions.</li></ol>',
 ['Keep the factor one half.','Use the given squared radius only once.','The common boundary may switch at an intersection.','A chord can require a triangle correction or its own radial equation.'],
 [
 example('E-A1','1 · One loop counted once',r'<p>Find the area of \(r=5\sin2\theta\), \(0\le\theta\le\pi/2\).</p>',r'\[A=\frac{25}{2}\int_0^{\pi/2}\sin^22\theta\,d\theta.\]<p>The double-angle identity gives</p>\[A=\frac{25}{4}\left[\theta-\frac{\sin4\theta}{4}\right]_0^{\pi/2}=\frac{25\pi}{8}.\]<p>The radius is non-negative on this interval and the loop is traced once.</p>'),
 example('E-A2','2 · A polynomial spiral sector',r'<p>Find the area bounded by \(r=2\theta\), \(0\le\theta\le2\), and its endpoint rays.</p>',r'\[A=\frac12\int_0^2(2\theta)^2\,d\theta=\frac23[\,\theta^3\,]_0^2=\frac{16}{3}.\]<p>The final ray is at angle 2 radians. There is no reason to replace this limit by a familiar fraction of a turn.</p>'),
 example('E-A3','3 · A common area with two integrals',r'<p>Find the common area of the circles \(r=4\cos\theta\) and \(r=4\sin\theta\).</p>',r'<p>The common interior is in the first quadrant. The non-pole intersection is at \(\theta=\pi/4\); the smaller radius is \(4\sin\theta\) before it and \(4\cos\theta\) after it.</p>\[A=8\int_0^{\pi/4}\sin^2\theta\,d\theta+8\int_{\pi/4}^{\pi/2}\cos^2\theta\,d\theta.\]\[A=2\pi-4.\]<p>The common pole is included, but its different angular representations are not extra area.</p>'),
 example('E-A4','4 · A chord cuts a circular cap',r'<p>Inside the circle \(r=6\), find the area above the line \(y=3\).</p>',r'<p>The chord meets the circle at angles \(\pi/6\) and \(5\pi/6\). The central angle is \(2\pi/3\), so the circular sector has area \(12\pi\). The triangle between the two radii has area \(9\sqrt3\). Hence</p>\[A=12\pi-9\sqrt3.\]<p>The same result follows from</p>\[A=\frac12\int_{\pi/6}^{5\pi/6}\left(36-9\csc^2\theta\right)\,d\theta.\]')
 ]),
lesson('extrema','Distances, tangents and extreme points',
 'Choose the distance function, differentiate the correct parametrisation and check all candidates.',
 'Find radial and Cartesian extrema, distinguishing horizontal tangents from singular parameter points.',
 r'<p>Review <a href="/courses/maths4u-9231-fp1/topics/fp1_polar_areas">polar parametrisations</a>, differentiation, stationary points and endpoint checks.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Choose the quantity being maximised</h3><p>Distance from the pole is r. Distance from the full x-axis is \(|y|=|r\sin\theta|\), and distance from the full y-axis is \(|x|=|r\cos\theta|\). For the full line through the pole at angle \(\alpha\), it is \(|r\sin(\theta-\alpha)|\). Squaring a non-negative distance often removes an inconvenient square root without changing its extrema.</p>
 <h3>Differentiate the coordinate, not just the radius</h3>
 \[\frac{dx}{d\theta}=r'\cos\theta-r\sin\theta,\]
 \[\frac{dy}{d\theta}=r'\sin\theta+r\cos\theta.\]
 <p>For a regular non-pole point, a horizontal tangent requires \(dy/d\theta=0\) and \(dx/d\theta\ne0\). If both derivatives vanish, the quotient alone does not determine the tangent; inspect a limit or the local parametrisation.</p>
 <h3>Retain every admissible candidate</h3><p>Solve the appropriate derivative equation on the full domain, including separate loops or lobes. Compare endpoint values and all stationary values. For a distance from a full axis, compare both signs through the absolute value or its square. Check a cancelled factor before discarding its roots.</p>
 <h3>Numerical roots need a complete argument</h3><p>Use a sign-changing bracket and retain enough digits until the final rounding. If the derivative is monotone on the bracket, justify uniqueness. For several branches compare their candidate distances, rather than selecting the first root found.</p>''',
 r'<ol><li>Write the required distance or coordinate explicitly.</li><li>Differentiate, keeping the original domain.</li><li>Find every stationary candidate and test endpoints or singular points.</li><li>Compare distances, then give all coordinates and angles requested.</li></ol>',
 ['Maximising r does not maximise every Cartesian distance.','Distance from a full axis uses an absolute value.','A zero numerator in dy/dx is insufficient when its denominator is also zero.','Keep unrounded roots until the final answer.'],
 [
 example('E-E1','1 · A radial maximum away from the endpoints',r'<p>For \(r=2+4\theta-\theta^2\), \(0\le\theta\le3\), find the greatest distance from the pole.</p>',r'<p>The radius is positive throughout. Its derivative is \(4-2\theta\), so the only interior stationary angle is 2. The endpoint radii are 2 and 5; the stationary radius is 6. Therefore the furthest point has polar coordinates \((6,2)\).</p>'),
 example('E-E2','2 · Maximise height on a circle',r'<p>For \(r=10\cos\theta\), \(0\le\theta\le\pi/2\), find the greatest distance from the x-axis and the corresponding polar point.</p>',r'\[y=10\cos\theta\sin\theta=5\sin2\theta.\]<p>The maximum is 5 at \(\theta=\pi/4\). At this angle the radius is \(5\sqrt2\), giving \((r,\theta)=(5\sqrt2,\pi/4)\). Both endpoint heights are zero.</p>'),
 example('E-E3','3 · Check a horizontal tangent is regular',r'<p>For \(r=4\sin2\theta\), \(0\le\theta\le\pi/2\), find the non-pole horizontal tangent and its height.</p>',r'\[y=8\sin^2\theta\cos\theta,\qquad y^\prime=8\sin\theta(2-3\sin^2\theta).\]<p>At the non-pole solution, \(\sin^2\theta=2/3\), so</p>\[\theta=\arctan\sqrt2,\qquad r=\frac{8\sqrt2}{3},\qquad y=\frac{16}{3\sqrt3}.\]<p>Here \(x^\prime=-8\sqrt3/3\ne0\), confirming a horizontal tangent. Endpoint heights vanish, so this is also the greatest height of the loop.</p>'),
 example('E-E4','4 · A unique numerical height maximum',r'<p>For \(r=5/(3+\theta)\), \(0\le\theta\le\pi/2\), find the greatest height to three decimal places.</p>',
 rf'<p>Differentiate \(y=5\sin\theta/(3+\theta)\). Its derivative has the sign of</p>\[g(\theta)=(3+\theta)\cos\theta-\sin\theta.\]<p>Since \(g^\prime(\theta)=-(3+\theta)\sin\theta&lt;0\) in the interior, while \(g(0)&gt;0\) and \(g(\pi/2)&lt;0\), there is exactly one maximum. Bisection gives \(\theta\approx{phi:.6f}\), and the greatest height is \({height:.3f}\). Use the unrounded angle in the substitution.</p>')
 ])
]
